    section: "B", questionRef: "2017 HL Section B Q1(d)",
    stem: "Rising levels of overweight and obesity are placing an increasing burden on individuals and society. Currently, six in ten adults and one in four children are overweight or obese. (A Healthy Weight for Ireland: Obesity Policy and Action Plan 2016 – 2025) Body Mass Index (BMI) is a standardised measure used to estimate whether or not someone is underweight, normal weight, overweight or obese.",
    questionText: "Assess the effects of high sugar consumption on the body.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"Three effects:4+3+3"}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "effects", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "effects", marks: 10, contextNote: "Choose ANY THREE distinct effects from the complete original pool. The first credited effect earns4, the next3, the next3; no fixed effect receives the four-mark award. Original 2017 scientific wording retained.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":3,"perOption":4,"options":["Obesity: foods high in sugar are often calorie dense and nutrient poor, can lead to weight gain; etc.","High blood pressure: eating foods high in fructose raises blood pressure; etc.","Heart disease: overconsumption of refined carbohydrates especially those high in sugar e.g. cakes and pastries can result in excess insulin production which can raise LDLs; etc.","Type 2 diabetes: excess sugar consumption leads to an increased risk for diabetes; normal blood sugar levels are between 4.0 – 7.9mmol/l; if blood sugars drop below this range hypoglycaemia can occur; if blood sugars rise above this range hyperglycaemia can occur; etc.","Dental disease: sugar causes tooth decay, it interacts with bacteria to produce acid; etc.","Nutritional deficiency: when too much sugar is consumed, other foods that provide important nutrients, such as fruits and vegetables may be lacking in the diet; etc."],"perOptionSteps":[4,3,3]} },
    ],
