    id: "he-2017-hl-sb-q3a", topicId: "home-economics-0-11", conceptId: "sb-q3a",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2017 HL Section B Q3(a)",
    stem: "The ‘taste’ experience is an accumulation of multiple senses.",
    questionText: "Discuss the influence of the senses when choosing, cooking and eating food.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"Choosing4 + cooking4 + eating4 + other4"}, totalMarks: 16,
    rows: [
    { id: "heading-1", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Choosing", marks: 4, contextNote: "Choosing: Sight is used to judge appearance - colour, size, shape; presentation e.g. garnish and serving; we expect certain foods to have a certain colour e.g. apples; flavour is associated with colour e.g. lemon - yellow, strawberry - red, mint - green; appearance and colour used to judge freshness when choosing food e.g. mould on Stilton cheese but not on bread; smell can be used to determine if food is fresh, rancid or poisonous; shape, size and surface appearance can determine whether to accept or reject a food; etc.", permittedMarks: [0,4] },
    { id: "heading-2", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Cooking", marks: 4, contextNote: "Cooking: Colour change due to cooking; cooking intensifies flavours - more appetising than raw food; doneness of food – under / over cooked, rare; cooking develops flavours - fruity, pungent, roasted, smoky, sour, spicy, strong; aroma develops; texture changes; sounds associated with cooking i.e. sizzling sausages; etc.", permittedMarks: [0,4] },
    { id: "heading-3", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Eating", marks: 4, contextNote: "Eating: Taste buds — bitter, sour, sweet and salty; nerves in the skin of the mouth are stimulated by thermal or chemical reactions e.g. coldness of ice cream or burning of chilli; mouth feel helps us detect the texture of food; descriptors - acidic, bitter, bland, creamy, piquant, salty, sharp, smoky, sour, spicy, sweet, tangy, tasteless; texture - consistency, roughness, smoothness of the food; detected by mouth feel; hearing / sound when eating - used to determine freshness of food e.g. crunchy vegetables or biscuits; descriptors - bubbling, crackling, crunchy, fizzy, snapping; etc.", permittedMarks: [0,4] },
    { id: "other", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "1 other", marks: 4, contextNote: "One ADDITIONAL point from any of the three required headings. Choosing: Sight is used to judge appearance - colour, size, shape; presentation e.g. garnish and serving; we expect certain foods to have a certain colour e.g. apples; flavour is associated with colour e.g. lemon - yellow, strawberry - red, mint - green; appearance and colour used to judge freshness when choosing food e.g. mould on Stilton cheese but not on bread; smell can be used to determine if food is fresh, rancid or poisonous; shape, size and surface appearance can determine whether to accept or reject a food; etc. Cooking: Colour change due to cooking; cooking intensifies flavours - more appetising than raw food; doneness of food – under / over cooked, rare; cooking develops flavours - fruity, pungent, roasted, smoky, sour, spicy, strong; aroma develops; texture changes; sounds associated with cooking i.e. sizzling sausages; etc. Eating: Taste buds — bitter, sour, sweet and salty; nerves in the skin of the mouth are stimulated by thermal or chemical reactions e.g. coldness of ice cream or burning of chilli; mouth feel helps us detect the texture of food; descriptors - acidic, bitter, bland, creamy, piquant, salty, sharp, smoky, sour, spicy, sweet, tangy, tasteless; texture - consistency, roughness, smoothness of the food; detected by mouth feel; hearing / sound when eating - used to determine freshness of food e.g. crunchy vegetables or biscuits; descriptors - bubbling, crackling, crunchy, fizzy, snapping; etc.", permittedMarks: [0,4] },
    ],
